"# Appendix A — Reading the Error

This is the honest-floor companion to Chapter 8 of the Book of
Introspection. Chapter 8 tells you what an error *could* be in the
fullest design: a rich record with a `:kind`, a `:did-you-mean`, a
`:cause` chain. This appendix tells you what an error *actually is*
when you run Motoi today — and it is rougher than the design. Reading
errors well means reading the rough truth, not the brochure.

Everything in this appendix was produced by actually running it. If a
message looks strange, it is because Motoi really says that. The point
of the Book of Limits is to speak the truth at the edges, and the edge
of the error system is one of the most useful truths a programmer — or
a small model writing code for one — can hold.

## A.1 The first question: did a verb error at all?

You would think this is obvious. It is not. Motoi's core is
*permissive*: it would rather coerce your arguments than stop you. So
the first skill is telling a real failure apart from a silent surprise.

There are exactly three outcomes when you run a form:

1. **A value.** The line prints a result. The program ran to the end.
2. **A stop.** A line begins with `error:`. Evaluation halted there;
   nothing after it ran.
3. **A surprise.** A value prints — but it is not the value you meant.
   No `error:` line. The language coerced, truncated, or shrugged.

Outcome 3 is the dangerous one, and it is the one this appendix exists
for. Consider:

```scheme
(string-length 42)
;; => 2
```

That is not an error. Motoi turned `42` into the string `\"42\"` and
measured it. Two characters. The program is *wrong* and the language
*said nothing*. Same shape here:

```scheme
(+ 1 'a)
;; => \"1a\"
```

`+` did not refuse a symbol. It concatenated. And here:

```scheme
(/ 5 0)
;; => Infinity
```

Division by zero is not a raised error in Motoi — it is IEEE floating
point, and it hands you `Infinity`. And the emptiest surprise of all:

```scheme
(car '())
;; =>
```

`car` of the empty list produces *nothing* — no value, no error. The
line is blank.

**The lesson of the honest floor:** a program that produced a value did
not necessarily succeed. When a result looks wrong, do not assume the
language would have stopped you. It often will not. This is why the
disciplined reflex is not \"read the error\" but \"wrap and check\" —
see A.6.

## A.2 The clean errors — the ones that read like Scheme

When Motoi *does* stop, most of the time it stops cleanly, with a
message that reads like a language talking to you. These are the ones
worth memorizing, because they cover the great majority of real
mistakes.

**`unbound symbol: <name>`**

```scheme
(frobnicate 3)
;; error: unbound symbol: frobnicate
```

You used a name Motoi does not know. Three real causes, in order of
likelihood: (1) a typo — you meant `cdr` and wrote `crd`; (2) the verb
lives in a library that has not been loaded (an SRFI part you did not
pull in); (3) the name genuinely does not exist in the language. This
is the single most common error, and the friendliest — it names exactly
what it could not find.

**`not a function: <value>`**

```scheme
(1 2 3)
;; error: not a function: 1
```

You put something in the head position of a call that is not callable.
Here, the literal `1`. This almost always means a misplaced paren — you
wrote `(1 2 3)` when you meant `'(1 2 3)` (a *quoted* list), or you
shadowed a verb name with a number and then tried to call it.

**`fuel exhausted`**

```scheme
(let loop ((n 0)) (loop (+ n 1)))
;; error: fuel exhausted
```

The evaluator has a step budget. A loop with no base case, or a
recursion that does not tail-call, burns through it and stops. This is
a *safety* stop, not a logic complaint: it means \"this would have run
forever, so I stopped it.\" The fix is almost never 'more fuel' — it is
a missing base case or an accumulator that never converges.

**`macro fuel exhausted`**

A separate budget, for macro *expansion* rather than evaluation. It
fires when a macro expands into more macro calls without bottoming out
— a fork-bomb-shaped macro. It is kept distinct from eval fuel on
purpose: a merely long-running program should not be mistaken for a
macro attack, and vice versa.

**Your own message, from `(error ...)`**

```scheme
(error \"custom boom\" 1 2)
;; error: custom boom 1 2
```

When you call R7RS `error` yourself, the message is your string
followed by your *irritants* (the extra values). This is the one error
whose text you fully control — so control it well. A good message names
the verb, the bad value, and what was expected.

## A.3 The leaky errors — when a host error escapes

Here is the part the brochure does not print. Motoi runs on
JavaScript, and its core verbs are thin. When you hand a verb an
argument so wrong that its implementation trips *before* any Scheme-level
check, you do not get a clean Scheme error. You get the raw host error,
leaking straight through:

```scheme
(car)
;; error: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading '0')
```

`car` with no argument reached for its first argument's head, found no
argument at all, and JavaScript complained about reading a property of
`undefined`. Another:

```scheme
(cdr 7)
;; error: a.slice is not a function
```

`cdr` of `7` tried to call a list operation on a number. The number has
no `.slice`, and the host said so. And:

```scheme
(assoc 1)
;; error: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'find')
```

`assoc` with a missing second argument reached for a list that was not
there.

**How to recognize a leaky error:** the message does not read like
Scheme. It talks about *properties*, *undefined*, `.slice`, `.find`,
`reading '0'`. Those words are never part of your program. When you see
them, translate: *a verb received an argument so far outside its
contract that its guts failed before it could tell you politely.*

The *meaning* is almost always the same as a type error: you passed the
wrong shape, or too few arguments. The leaky message is uglier but the
fix is the same — look at the verb, look at what you handed it, and fix
the shape. A.4 shows how the census quietly cleans these up for you.

**Why this matters for the honest floor:** a leaky error is Motoi
admitting an incomplete edge. It is not lying to you — it is showing
you its seams. A programmer who knows the seams is faster than one who
expected a polished record and got confused by `.slice`.

## A.4 The error card — what the census remembers

Every error that *escapes* to the top level (see A.6 for what
'escapes' means) is filed. The filing system is the error census, and
each filed error is, in effect, a small card:

```scheme
(stats/error-counts 5)
;; => ((:unbound-symbol 11 real)
;;     (:class:TypeError 4 provisional)
;;     (:not-a-procedure 2 provisional)
;;     (:fuel-exhausted 1 provisional))
```

Read a card left to right:

- **The key** — a *normalized* name for the error. This is the
  census's gift: the ugly leaky messages from A.3 all collapse into one
  tidy key, `class:TypeError`. The census did the translation you would
  otherwise do by eye. Clean errors keep clean keys: `unbound-symbol`,
  `not-a-procedure`, `fuel-exhausted`.
- **The count** — how many times this exact shape of failure has been
  seen, across every session, stored on disk (never in the model's
  weights). Eleven unbound symbols. Facts live in the counter.
- **The status** — `provisional` or `real`. A brand-new failure mode is
  `provisional`; once it has recurred enough to be a *pattern* rather
  than a fluke, it is promoted to `real`. This is the census deciding
  which wrongs are worth building a lesson around.

The summary card gives the whole shape at a glance:

```scheme
(stats/error-summary)
;; => ((:distinct-errors 4) (:total-errors 18) (:real 1)
;;     (:provisional 3) (:novel-this-session 0)
;;     (:errors-this-session 0) (:stats-slat \"/Users/you/.motoi/stats.slat\"))
```

Four distinct shapes of failure, eighteen failures in total, one shape
mature enough to be `real`. And it names the file: the whole census is
a plain `.slat` file on disk. You can open it. It is not a black box.

The card in this section is a metaphor — but Motoi keeps the *literal*
cards too. `scheme/reference/error-cards/` is a whole shelf, one hand-
authored card per KIND of failure the census counts: what the wrong
means, how to recognize it at a verb, how to detect it in code, and the
honest fix. Where this appendix reads *one* count, a card reads the
coordinate behind it — `class:TypeError` has a card that explains why a
leaky host error (A.3) lands there and not in `not-a-procedure`. Each
card's example is run through `./bin/motoi` and reproduces its output
verbatim; nothing aspirational ships. When the census promotes a
`provisional` key to `real`, that is the signal a card is owed — and the
mint script (`error-cards/scripts/mint-error-cards.mjs`) scaffolds the
stub for a human to fill. Boot the shelf at `error-cards/README.slatl`.

## A.5 What a single verb's failures look like

To ask 'how often has *this specific* failure happened?', name the key:

```scheme
(stats/error-count-of \"unbound-symbol\")
;; => 11
```

And to turn that into a *health signal* — a fraction of all failures —
divide by the total:

```scheme
(define (error-share key)
  (let ((total (cadr (assoc :total-errors (stats/error-summary))))
        (c (stats/error-count-of key)))
    (if (> total 0)
        (exact->inexact (/ c total))
        0)))
(error-share \"unbound-symbol\")
;; => 0.611...
```

Sixty-one percent of everything that ever went wrong was an unbound
symbol. That is a *finding*. It says: the most valuable thing this
system could do to help its users is catch typos and missing imports
before they run. The census turned a pile of failures into a priority.

## A.6 The reliable way to tell a verb errored: wrap it

Reading `error:` off a screen is for humans at a REPL. A *program* — or
a model writing one — needs a mechanical answer. The answer is `guard`,
Scheme's standard error trap:

```scheme
(guard (e (#t 'it-errored))
  (frobnicate 3))
;; => it-errored
```

If the clause fires, the body errored. If it does not, the body
returned. That is the whole test. Wrap it into a helper that never
throws and always tells you which way it went:

```scheme
(define (safe-call thunk)
  (guard (e (#t (list :err (if (error-object? e)
                               (error-object-message e)
                               e))))
    (list :ok (thunk))))

(list (safe-call (lambda () (+ 1 2)))
      (safe-call (lambda () (error \"no price\" 42))))
;; => ((:ok 3) (:err \"no price\"))
```

`(:ok value)` means it ran; `(:err message)` means it stopped. Now
failure is a *value* you can branch on, collect, or count — not a
screen you have to read.

**A crucial subtlety — guard hides errors from the census.** An error
you catch with `guard` is *handled*. It never escaped to the top level,
so the census does **not** count it:

```scheme
(guard (e (#t 'handled)) (car))   ;; census unchanged
(car)                              ;; census +1 class:TypeError
```

This is correct, not a bug. The census is a record of failures that
actually *stopped a program*. A failure you anticipated and handled is
not a failure of the system — it is the system working. So: catch what
you can handle, and let the rest escape, where the census can learn
from it.

## A.7 `error-object-message` and `error-object-irritants`

When you catch a *clean* error (one raised by `error`), you can read its
parts. Two R7RS accessors do the work:

```scheme
(guard (e (#t (error-object-message e)))
  (error \"bad price\" 42 'widget))
;; => \"bad price\"

(guard (e (#t (error-object-irritants e)))
  (error \"bad price\" 42 'widget))
;; => (42 widget)
```

The message is your headline; the irritants are the values you attached.
Guard the accessor behind `error-object?`, though — a *raised* value is
not always an error object. You can `raise` any value at all:

```scheme
(guard (e (#t e))
  (raise (list (list :kind 'shop/empty-cart)
               (list :cart 'welcome))))
;; => ((:kind shop/empty-cart) (:cart welcome))
```

That is not an error object — it is a plain alist you chose to raise. So
`(error-object? e)` would be `#f`, and you would read it with `assoc`
instead. The rule: **`error-object?` first, then the right accessor.**

## A.8 The taxonomy, in one table

Fix this table in memory. It is the whole error surface of Motoi as it
runs today.

| What you see                                   | Census key         | What it means                                     |
|------------------------------------------------|--------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `error: unbound symbol: X`                     | `unbound-symbol`   | Typo, or a library not loaded, or no such name.   |
| `error: not a function: X`                     | `not-a-procedure`  | Non-callable in head position; usually a paren.   |
| `error: fuel exhausted`                        | `fuel-exhausted`   | Runaway loop / non-tail recursion. Missing base.  |
| `error: macro fuel exhausted`                  | `macro-fuel-*`     | A macro expanded without bottoming out.           |
| `error: <your text> <irritants>`              | (varies)           | Your own `(error ...)`. You control this message. |
| `error: Cannot read properties of undefined…`  | `class:TypeError`  | Leaky host error: arg too wrong / too few. A.3.   |
| `error: a.slice is not a function`             | `class:TypeError`  | Leaky host error: non-list to a list verb.        |
| *(a value, but the wrong one)*                 | *(none)*           | Silent coercion. The language did not stop you.    |
| *(a blank line)*                               | *(none)*           | Silent nothing, e.g. `(car '())`.                 |

The top four rows are clean and enumerable. The two `class:TypeError`
rows are the leaky seam. The bottom two rows are the honest floor — the
failures the language will *not* announce.

## A.9 The reflex, stated once

- If a verb printed a value, do not trust it blindly — a wrong value is
  a real possibility, because the core coerces. When it matters, wrap
  and assert the *shape* of the result, not just its presence.
- If a verb stopped with an `error:` line that reads like Scheme, read
  the key from A.8 and fix the named thing.
- If a verb stopped with a message about `undefined` or `.slice` or
  `.find`, translate it: you handed a verb a shape it could not use.
  The census already filed it under `class:TypeError`.
- To detect failure mechanically, `guard` and return a tagged value.
- To learn from failure across time, let it escape and read the census.

That is reading the error. It is not a polished record you project
fields from — not yet. It is a rough surface you learn to read fast.
Knowing the rough surface is what separates a programmer who is fast in
Motoi from one who is confused by it. And for the model reading this:
the same knowledge is what lets you write code that *fails legibly* —
raise clean errors with `error` and good messages, so the failures your
users hit land in the top four rows of A.8, not the leaky middle.

Turn to Appendix B for what to do when the census has *nothing* to say
— the honest miss.
"
)
